The `isSupported()` function from push-client.js checks:
```javascript
export function isSupported() {
  return (
    typeof window !== "undefined" &&
    "serviceWorker" in navigator &&
    "PushManager" in window &&
    "Notification" in window
  );
}
```

On iOS Safari (browser mode, not standalone PWA), `PushManager` is only available:
- iOS 16.4+ AND the app must be added to home screen (standalone)
- Before iOS 16.4, `PushManager` is not in window at all even in Safari
So the support matrix depends on iOS version and whether the PWA is in standalone mode—on older iOS or in browser mode, the check fails, but on iOS 16.4+ in standalone mode it should work, though I'm still clarifying whether push notifications actually deliver in browser mode or only when the PWA is installed to the home screen. the global scope, but subscriptions only work in standalone mode. Since the user says it's still reporting unsupported, one of the three APIs—`serviceWorker`, `PushManager`, or `Notification`—isn't available in their browser. For iOS users specifically, I should improve the error messaging to clarify that push notifications require iOS 16.4+ and the app must be installed to the home screen, not just accessed through Safari. Updating both layouts...

