That's the key clue — same password works on Pixel/Chrome but not iPhone Safari. That rules out a wrong password and points to something Safari-specific. Look at the login code again:

```js
const data = await res.json();
localStorage.setItem("token", data.token);   // ← if THIS throws, we jump to catch
...
} catch {
  errEl.textContent = "Invalid email or password. Please try again.";
}
```

The login request itself likely **succeeds** on your iPhone, but `localStorage.setItem` throws — which happens in **Safari Private Browsing** (and some iOS tracking-prevention setups) — and the catch block then mislabels it as "invalid password." On the Pixel, localStorage works, so it goes through. Let me confirm the surrounding code and fix the error handling so storage failures don't masquerade as bad credentials.

